      "question": "There's an open corrective work order on Transformer 1 at site MAIN for the gassing problem, and the crew says the unit has settled down. I want to check that before we close it.\n\nFind the open corrective work order on Transformer 1 that relates to gassing, discharge, overheating or oil treatment. Give me its number, description, priority, failure code and assigned technician. If there's more than one, work with the oldest and list the rest.\n\nThen build two separate findings from the samples between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000:\n1. Screen hydrogen, ethylene and acetylene for anomalous samples with a proper anomaly detection model. List any flagged timestamps with their values.\n2. Run a dissolved gas fault classification on the first and the last complete sample, using hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene. Tell me whether the class stayed the same, moved to a lower-energy or lower-temperature fault, or got worse.\n\nDecide as follows:\n- Close the work order only if both findings say it's calm: nothing is flagged in the last two samples, and the last class is the same as or milder than the first. Put both findings in the resolution notes and keep the existing failure code.\n- If exactly one finding points to trouble, don't close it. Add a note saying which check disagreed, and recommend a confirmatory oil resample within 7 days.\n- If both findings point to trouble, keep it open and raise its priority by one level, but not above 1.\n\nCount a sample as complete only if all five gases are non-null. If any of the three screened gases has fewer than 4 non-null samples, skip the model for that gas and flag any sample more than 50% above the gas's window mean as a manual outlier. If there's no matching open work order at all, say so, report both findings, and don't create anything.",
      "answer": "I did not close work order **1000052**. The dissolved gas check came back calm, but I couldn't run the anomaly model, so I can't confirm the unit has settled.\n\n**Work order**\n- **Number:** 1000052 (corrective, status APPR, reported 2026-09-30)\n- **Description:** \"Transformer 1 - oil sample retest (DGA) and insulation check for T2 thermal fault\"\n- **Priority:** 3\n- **Failure code:** FC006 (overheating)\n- **Assigned technician:** none. No technician is assigned, and the work order notes say no one has prior work history on this unit.\n- **Other open work order:** 1000051, \"OLTC inspection \u2013 on-load tap changer\" (P1, WAPPR). It's a PdM work order, not corrective, so it doesn't count as a second match.\n\n**Finding 1 \u2013 Anomaly screen: not run**\n- There are 4 samples in the window (Jan 15, 16, 17 and 18), and all five gases are present in each. With 4 values per gas, your rule says to use the model, not the manual 50% check.\n- The model couldn't be run:\n  - The model catalog has no anomaly detection models.\n  - The recipe tool needs a data file, and I have no tool to create one from the sensor readings.\n  - Earlier anomaly runs on this asset don't record which data they used, so I didn't rely on them.\n- **For information only (this doesn't replace the model):** no sample is more than 50% above its window mean.\n\n| Gas | Values (Jan 15 / 16 / 17 / 18, ppm) | Mean | Highest vs. mean |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hydrogen | 2845 / 2950 / 2800 / 2900 | 2873.75 | +2.7% |\n| Ethylene | 16684 / 16500 / 16700 / 16600 | 16621 | +0.5% |\n| Acetylene | 7 / 6 / 8 / 7 | 7 | +14% |\n\n**Finding 2 \u2013 Dissolved gas fault class: stayed the same (calm)**\n- The classification tool returned \"LLM unavailable\" three times, so I worked out the IEC 60599 ratios by hand.\n- **First sample (Jan 15):** acetylene/ethylene = 0.0004, methane/hydrogen = 2.60, ethylene/ethane = 3.05. That's a T2 thermal fault (300\u2013700 \u00b0C).\n- **Last sample (Jan 18):** acetylene/ethylene = 0.0004, methane/hydrogen = 2.53, ethylene/ethane = 3.05. Also T2.\n- The class didn't change, so it hasn't got worse.\n\n**Decision:** Nothing was changed: the work order isn't closed, no note was added and the priority stays at 3. Closing needs both findings to be calm, and the first one is missing. Neither finding points to trouble, so the \"note\" and \"raise priority\" branches don't apply either.\n\nTo finish the check, the three gases for Jan 15\u201318 need to be saved as a CSV file, or a detector added to the model catalog. Then I can run the screen and apply your rules.",
      "score": {
